Titre$ L E R A T I O M Y C E S   -   NOORDELOOS (2011)
Com$ STROPHARIACEAE  -  adapté par Paul Bertéa 31-10-2013
1,2 $ Pileus orange-red to red finally red-brown
1,3 $ Pileus pale brown, pale yellow-brown or ochre, sometimes with ochre-brown centre
2,0 $ Pileus viscid when moist; chrysocystidia abundant.. $ L. ceres
2,0 $ Pileus more or less dry; chrysocystidia absent $ L. squamosus var. thraustus
3,4 $ Stipe without annulus, but at most with a fibrillose annuliform zone
3,5 $ Stipe with distinct membranous annulus; spores with central germ pore
4,0 $ Large species, pileus 25-80 mm broad, pale brown, yellow-brown, or orange-ochraceous. growing on woodchips etc.; spores (12.5-) 13.0-16.0 (-16.5) x 7.0-9.0 µm, oval in profile view. with distinctly eccentric germ pore $ L. percevalii
4,0 $ Small species, pileus 10-30 mm broad, bright yellow. growing on grass remnants in gras­slands and open. ruderal places; spores 10.0-14.0 x 7.0-9.0 x 6.0-8.0 µm. flattened, so­metimes hexagonal in profile view with central germ-pore $ L. laetissimus
5,0 $ Lamellae with olivaceous tinge when mature; spores relatively small, 11.0-13.5 (-14.5) x (6.0-) 6.5-8.5 (-9.0) µm $ L. squamosus var. squamosus
5,0 $ Lamellae without olivaceous tinge when mature; spores larger, 12.0-16.5 x 7.0-10.0 µm $ L. magnivelaris
